Earthquake in Indonesia, tsunami killed, death toll rises
Officials said on Saturday that when a huge earthquake and tsunami occurred on the Indonesian island of Sulawessi, there was a lighting wave in the sea and at least 384 people died.
A festival was being celebrated on the beach of Palu city on Friday in which hundreds of people had gathered, when the six-meters (18-feet) high waves rose after the wave rise, it wreaked havoc, which killed many people and ruined everything around them.
The tsunami occurred after a 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
"Tomorrow when the tsunami was risked, people on the beach were engaged in their activities and were not in a position to get out immediately," Sutopo Purvo Nugroho, a spokesman for Indonesia's disaster mitigation agency BNPB in Briefing in Jakarta said.
"Tsunami not only became the reason for the people's deaths, but it also wrapped cars, logs, and houses. Before attacking the beach, the tsunami traveled at a speed of 800 kmph (497 mph).
To avoid the tsunami, some people climbed trees and defended themselves, he said.
16,700 people were evacuated from 24 centres in Palu.
Photographs posted from the plane by the Disaster Agency showed that many buildings and shops have been ruined, bridges have been twisted and collapsed and a mosque is also engulfed by water.
On Saturday, the subsequent blow in the coastal city continued on the cliffs.
2.2 million people in the area experienced a persistent earthquake.
Indonesia's Agency for the Assessment and Application of Technology (BPPT) said in a statement that the energy generated by a very large quake on Friday was about 200 times more than the capacity of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima in World War II.
It pointed out that the geography of the city at the end of a long, narrow bay can increase the size of a tsunami.
Nugroho elaborated on the loss, saying it was "very terrible" and that caused thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels to collapse.
The bodies of some people were trapped in the rubble of the collapsed buildings and 540 people were injured and 29 were not traced, he said.
The loss of lives and damage is very likely to occur in an area of Dongala, located 300 kilometers (190 miles) north of Palu near the epicentre of the quake, Nugroho said.
Nugroho pointed out that the communication system in Dongala was “completely damaged”.
In a statement, the Red Cross said that more than 300,000 people are living here and its staff and volunteers were visiting the affected areas.
“It’s already a tragic incident, but it can be even more terrible,” it added.
On Saturday, the agency criticized why it was not reported earlier if a tsunami was about to arrive in Palu, although officials pointed out that the warning was about to be issued but the waves had already arrived.
Made some videos amateurly by a person and posted on social media in which it can be seen positioning on the upper floor of the building was sounding a person on the street below and a frightening warning can be heard.
Within a few minutes, a lot of water started coming out from the middle and also dragged the buildings and cars with it.
Reuters wasn't able to immediately certify the footage.
Earthquakes and tsunamists led to power problems in Palu and communication lines were cut, making it harder for officials to link rescue efforts together.
With the help of Jakarta and other cities, officials have started sending cargo planes, officials said, adding that there is still a need to evacuate people and meet food as well as other requirements.
The city's airport has been reopened only for relief efforts and will be closed in October.
President Joko Widodo was scheduled to visit evacuation centres in Palu on Sunday.
