“On talking to him, he came to know that he saw a lot of houses being ruined,” he said.
It's very terrible.
Support groups seriously started the work of bringing goods for disaster relief, with some people complaining that external support personnel having special expertise are being prohibited from coming to Palu.
According to Indonesia's rules, funds from abroad, any goods and employees can be helped only if the disaster zone is declared as a national disaster zone.
However, this has not been done yet.
"This disaster is still at the province level," said Aulia Ariani, a spokeswoman for the Red Cross in Indonesia.
"After the government says that it's okay, it's a national disaster, we can involve international support personnel in the rescue operation but it hasn't happened yet."
Even after the second night of Friday's quake and tsunami, people whose friends and family members were not traced in Palu hoped that their loved ones would miraculously be found somewhere as it happens after natural calamities.
It was found on Saturday that a small boy was trapped in the sewer.
