The U.S should double the funds to be given for A.I.
Kai-Fu Li, an investor who has reduced for Google, Microsoft and Apple, said, Since China is becoming more active in artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double the amount of expenditure incurred on research in this field.
After several U.S. government's actions, AI has made this comment, he also said that there is a lack of a comprehensive formal AI strategy in the U.S.
Meanwhile, China launched a plan last year: it aims to reach No. 1 in AI innovation by 2030.
"Doubling the AI research budget will give a great start, take a look at all other other countries, so the U.S. is far behind this matter, and we're trying for AI's next success," Lee said.
“Doubling the fund could double the possibility that AI’s next major achievement will take place in U.S.,” Lee told CNBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book "AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order" was published by Houghton Miflin Harcourt earlier this month, CEO of Sinovation Ventures, is an investor of Face+, one of China's finest AI companies.
In the 1980's, at Carnegie Mellon University, he worked on an AI system that surpassed the highest-ranked U.S. Othlo Player significantly, and was later appointed as an executive in Microsoft Research and president at Google's China branch.
Lee described the technical qualifications of the previous U.S. government, which includes the robot-related challenges of the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency and who would be the next government that would recognize the next visionary.
Lee pointed out that U.S. researchers often face a lot of difficulties in getting government help.
"It's not China to remove academic leaders, it's corporate," Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other tech companies have appointed giants from the university to work on AI.
Lee pointed out that by changing the immigration policy, the U.S can be helped in its AI efforts.
"""In my opinion, it should be allowed to do a PhD in AI through a green card on its own,"" he said."
In July 2017, China's State Council released its Next Generation Artificial Intelligence Development Plan.
“China’s National Natural Science Foundation gives financial grants to researchers of the National Science Foundation and other government organizations, but the quality of education work in China is not very good,” Lee said.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Defense Department opened a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, which aims to involve industry and academia partners, and the White House announced the creation of the Select Committee for Artificial Intelligence.
In addition, in the same month, DARPA launched an initiative under the name of AI Next with an investment of $2 billion.
For NSF, this investment currently accounts for more than $100 million every year in AI research.
Meanwhile, a law was enacted in the U.S. that the demand for the creation of a National Security Commission for Artificial Intelligence would be ignored for months.
The people of Macedonia voted for whether the country's name would be changed
On Sunday, the people of Macedonia voted for whether the country's name would be changed to "Republick of North Macedonia" is a task that would resolve the decades-old dispute with Greece, leading to a halt to its membership bids in the European Union and NATO.
There is also a province in Greece named Macedonia, which says its northern neighbor's name is on its territory, leading to two names for the same claim and is already involved in NATO and the European Union.
Both governments made a deal in June based on the suggested new name, but nationalist opponents opposed it and said renaming Macedonia would weaken the ethnic identities of the majority people living in Slavik.
President Göjorz Ivanov said that he will not go to the referendum and boycott the campaign, raising doubts that only a minimum of 50 per per cent of the votes required for this vote will be valid.
The question on the voting ballot will say: “Do you give your approval for an agreement with Greece for membership of NATO and the EU.” ”
Prime Minister Zoran Zev, as well as supporters associated with the change of name, say that it is necessary for Macedonia to become members in bodies such as the EU and NATO, one of the countries that has emerged from the collapse of Yugoslavia.
"Today I have come to vote for the country's future, this future is for young people in Macedonia, this will enable them to live freely under the umbrella of the EU because it means a safe life for all of us."
However, there is no legal insistence, with most of the Members of Parliament saying they will act according to the results of the vote to make it decisive.
A two-thirds majority is needed to change names in Parliament.
According to the State Election Commission, there was no information about the irregularities till 1 pm.
However, in 2016, when the last parliamentary elections were held, voting was only 16 per cent compared to 34 per cent, of which 66 per per cent of the registered voters voted.
Gojo Tanevsky, a 62-year-old voter living in the capital, Scopje, said, "I came to vote because of my children, our place is in Europe."
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zev along with his wife Zorika and his son Dushko went to Macedonia to cast their votes in Macedonia for a change in the country's nawn name in Stromica, Macedonia on September 30, 2018.
54-year-old Vladimir Kavardakov, who hails from Scopje in front of Parliament, was preparing a small stage and putting chairs in front of the tents of those who opposed the voting.
“We need the membership of NATO and the EU, but we want to get involved by raising our heads, not through an agreement,” Kavarkov said.
"""Our country is poor, but we also have our own self-respect."
If they don’t want us to subscribe to Macedonia, it doesn’t matter, we can become a part of Euro-Asia integration by connecting with other countries like China and Russia.””
Prime Minister Zev has said that getting membership in NATO will provide a much-needed investment for Macedonia, with an unemployment rate of more than 20 per cent.
"I think a lot of votes will gain positivity because more than 80 percent of our citizens agreed to join the EU and NATO.
He pointed out that the "yes" result will "confirm our future."
The survey, published by the Institute for Policy Research in Macedonia last Monday, showed 30 to 43 per per cent of the voters who will participate in the polls.
The second survey conducted by Telma TV of Macedonia on Sunday revealed that 57 percent of people are planning to vote.
Out of them, 70 percent of people agree to vote yes.
More than 50 per cent voting is necessary.
If the voting fails, the western government policy, to be formed in May last year, will be the first serious setback.
