John Edward: It is necessary to have language skills for citizens from outside countries
Scotland's private schools keep an eye on the track record of great education, and since 2018 it has continued to give the best exam results, thereby strengthening individual and collective success in sports, art, music, and other community endeavors.
More than 30,000 students are associated with The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) across Scotland, under their leadership these schools are trying to provide the best service to their students and parents.
The goal of private schools is to prepare their students for better and higher education, their chosen careers and their places are giving benefits to the citizens of foreign countries.
As an education sector that can build and implement a school curriculum, we are seeing that schools continue to choose modern languages as an alternative to the well-known and desired subject.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you talk to a person in a language that he understands, you'll be great for him.
If you talk to him in the same language, then his heart is very good.” ”
When we want to build a relationship and trust the people of other countries, we think we can rely more on English.
In this year's exam results, we can see that the languages in private schools are at the top of the league table with the highest passing rates.
68 per cent of them are students who have secured the highest Grade A in foreign languages.
Data collected by 74 SCIS member schools can see that 72 percent of students received the highest grade A in Mandarin language, while 72 percent of students in German, 69 percent of students in the French language and 63 per cent received Spanish Language Grade A.
This shows that private schools in Scotland are cooperating in foreign languages giving children and young people the skills they will need in the future.
Lingues are now being included in private school curriculum as an elective subject (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the same way as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics).
A survey conducted by the U.K. Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014 showed that employers struggled to hire employees in vacant positions, with 17 per cent candidates who lacked language skills.
Therefore, it is becoming imperative to have as much language skills as possible to prepare young people for their future career.
These skills are necessary to work anywhere in the world due to more potential employment opportunities in need of language.
No matter what choice is chosen for a career, if people have learned another language, then such skills will definitely benefit them in the future.
If you are able to talk directly to foreigners, being a multi-lingual person, you will automatically move forward in the competition.
In 2013, more than 4,000 adults from the UK participated in the YouGov survey, according to 75 per cent people could not speak enough in a foreign language, and the French was the only topic that 15 per cent people could speak and was the only language to reach double-digit percenties.
For this reason, it has become necessary to invest in teaching languages to children today.
By having knowledge of many languages, especially the languages of countries of developing economies, children will be able to get better opportunities to find the right jobs.
Languages taught in every school in Scotland will be different.
There are a lot of schools that will focus on more classic modern languages, while other schools will teach languages that have an attitude towards 2020 and are considered the most important for the UK, for example Mandarin or Japanese.
Even if your child is interested in learning any language, they will always be able to choose that language in private schools and they will also find staff who are experts in this field.
Private schools in Scotland are dedicated to a teaching environment that will prepare children for the future and give them the necessary skills for success, so that they can move forward in the future.
For a global business environment, it cannot be ruled out that it is very important to learn languages in the country for the future, so it should be given a better place in education.
In fact, modern languages must be truly accepted as “international communication skills”.
The choice of young people from Scotland to choose this option, diversity and excellence will continue to be given by private schools.
Il faut bien le faire.
The Director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools is John Edward
