Harvard students are taking courses for a job with a comfortable job
Harvard University has instituted a new course this year, which is for all its undergraduates who want to get more and more comfortable while competing in growing macho culture to study through caffeine-fueled 'All-Nighters'.
The teacher who teaches the students of the world's number one university often has no choice when there are absolutely common things about what to do to relax himself.
Charles Qazisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and an expert at Brigham and Women's Hospital, has created a course for which he believes is the first-of-its-kind course at the U.S.
After talking about the effect of less sleep at the time of teaching, he came up with the idea of starting this course.
She told The Telegraph, 'I had a girl at the end of the year and she said: 'Why do I say this every time I have to go to the next class next year?'
He told that no one told him about the importance of sleep – which shocked me.”
With the introduction of this course for the first time this year, students will come to know how the good habit of sleeping is very important in studies and athletic performances, besides improving their general health.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services, said that students do not sleep enough every week for which the university decided to start this course.
A series of interactive tasks have been included in the hours-long course.
In a section, there will be an image of a durmitory room, where students will click on coffee cups, curtains, trainers and books to explain what will have an impact on caffeine and light and how sleep-deprivation affects athletic performance, as well as the importance of bedtime in the routine.
In the second section, participants will be told that a lack of adequate sleep can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
The map of the campus with interactive icon will be shown, then participants will be encouraged to think about their daily routine.
“We are aware that this will not change the behaviour of the students immediately,” said Professor Zesseler.
However, we believe that they should have this information – just as the impact of smoking cigarettes on health.”” ”
He said that there is still a culture of feeling proud to 'wake up the whole rat night', modern technology and increasing pressure on students means that they have to sleep less which has become a growing problem.
"Enquiring whether you sleep enough and in a good sleep is like a ""secret weapon"" of pupils, it can make them cope with stress, exhaustion and anxiety,” he said, adding that it also protects against the impact on weight as a lack of sleep, their brain is always thinking about hunger, which makes them constantly hungry."
Reymond So, a 19-year-old from California who studied chemical and physical biology, did Professor Jesseler's help in the creation of the course, completing his last-year class at Harvard during last year.
He said that due to the course, he became aware and was inspired for the campus-wide course.
In the future, they are hopeful that it will be considered necessary for all postgraduate students to complete this course before joining a competitive institution.
Prof Jestler suggested that pupils should consider setting an alarm before going to sleep, at the same time, they should also decide when to wake up, and they should be aware of the detrimental effect of 'blue light' emanating from an electronic screen and LED light, which can put too many cirkadians on them, causing sleep problems.
