Almnack: The inventor of the geyser counter
Now a page of our "Sunday Morning" calendar: 136 years ago today, September 30, 1882 and the time after... the day when future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geyzer was born in Germany.
Geyser invented a device known as Geyser's Geysser Counter for the method of detecting and measuring radioactivity.
Geyser Counter is a mainstay of science, it has become the mainstay of pop culture, at the same time, a film "Bells of Coronado" in 1950, with the first-time scientist Roy Rogers and Dale Evans acting:
Man: "What does he do for the world?"
Rogers: "It's a geyser counter used to detect radioactive minerals like uranium.
After turning these earphones, you can definitely hear the effects of atoms hidden due to radioactivity in minerals."
Evans: "Tell me, it's popping for sure right now!"
"Hans" Geyser passed away in 1945, at that time he was going to be 63.
However, his name is still alive due to his invention.
