What is the Global Citizen Festival and does it reduce poverty?
New York, this Saturday, will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual concert in which many extremely influential stars are going to perform, and equally influential its mission; eliminating the poverty of the world.
Now in its seventh year, the Great Lawn of Central Park in the Global Citizen Festival will not only come up with tens of thousands of people enjoying artists like Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Sean Mendades, but will also work to raise awareness about the true goal of an extremely poverty eradication programme by 2030.
Beginning in 2012, the Global Citizen Festival is an extension of the Pro-Independence Group, the Global Poverty Project, which actively hopes to end poverty by increasing the number of people fighting against it.
To get a free ticket for the program (except when you're not ready to pay a VIP ticket), must meet "actions" in many ways such as actions such as to create awareness about your goal of eradicating poverty for the consert, emailing a world leader, making phone calls, or other meaningful methods.
But how successful the global citizen has been in the remaining 12 years to achieve his goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people through a free concert the right way to persuade people to call for action, or there's just another matter of so-called "clitivism" — to make people feel that they are making a truly difference by signing a petition or sending tweets?
Since 2011, the Global Citizen says it has recorded more than 19 million "actions" from its supporters while pursuing efforts for various goals.
It says these actions have helped world leaders to announce more than $37 billion worth of commitments and policies set to influence the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements stemming from its actions, of which at least $10 billions are already distributed or fundraising.
The group estimates that secured funds have had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world so far.
Some of the major commitments include The Power of Neutritions, a U.K.-based investors and participants who are committed to "helping children to grow to their entire potential, promising to provide Rwanda $35 million to end malnutrition in the county after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from global citizens."
"With the help of global citizens like the UK government, donors, national government, and you, we can make social injustice of malnutrition a footnote of history," said Tracy Ulaman, Ambassador of Power of Nutrition during a live concert in London in April 2018.
After more than 5,000 activities to the U.K. for improving nutrition for mothers and children, the government announced funding for a project called Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children with nutritional intervention.
In response to a frequently-asked question on your website "Whhy do you think we can eradicate excessive poverty?"
Global Citizen replied: "It will be a long and difficult way — sometimes we'll fall and fail."
But, similarly to great civil rights and anti-apartheid movements before us, we will succeed, since we are more powerful together.
Janet Jackson, The Weekend, Sean Mendes, Cardie B, Janell Monai are one of the few performers performing at this year's event in New York, hosted by Debra-Ley Furnace and Hugh Jackman.
