The inventor of the World Wide Web is planning to launch a new internet compared to Google and Facebook.
World Wide Web inventor Tim Berners-Lee has started a startup that wants to compete with Facebook, Amazon, and Google.
Technology Legend's latest project, Inrupt, is a company that builds Burners-Lee's open source platform Solid.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people will be allowed to access information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast company, Berners-Lee jokingly said the intention behind Imprupt was "world domination."
"We have to do it right now," he said about the startup.
"It's a historic moment."
The app uses Soldi's technology to allow people to create their own "Personal Online Data Store" or POD.
This may include contact lists, to-do lists, calendars, music libraries, and other personal and business tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available on one browser and at the same time.
The unique thing about the personal online data store is that it depends entirely on the user who can access what kind of information.
The company calls it "Personal Empowering Through Data."
According to the company's CEO John Bruce, the idea of Inrupt is to bring resources, processes, and proper skills by the company so that Solid can make available to everyone.
The company currently includes a security platform purchased by Burners-Lee, Bruce, IBM, a community of some on-staff developers contracted to work on the project.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Ley said that she and her team are "not talking to Facebook and Google about whether to introduce the entire change, where all of their business models will be completely exhausted overnight."
"We are not asking them for permission."
In a post on Medium published on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Imprupt's "Mission is to provide commercial energy and ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new web built on Solid."
In 1994, Burners-Lee replaced the internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Burners-Lee Net has been an impressive voice in the neutrality debate.
Even when launching Inrupt, Burners-Lee will continue to be the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic for this next era of the web," Berners-Lee said.
Bernard Vaughan: W.W.1 Victoria Crass Claric celebrated
The only pastor of the Church of England, who won the Victoria Cross as a warrior during World War I, was celebrated for 100 years in his home town.
Lt. Colonel Reverend Bernard Von won the award on September 29, 1918, in an attack at Belangalize and Lehocourt.
However, they were killed by a sniper four days later and he never knew that he had won the highest British military honour.
Monoument stone was unveiled by two of his grandsons at the parade held at Rushden, Northamptonshire on Saturday.
