According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lt. Colonel Von led his battalion at Canal de Saint-Quentin "amid a very dense fowl, heavy shelling on the battlefield and machine guns."
He later proceeded towards the firing line and with "large valour," led the military-line before taking field-guns in his hands alone and dashed three detachments.
On October 4, 1918, Lt. Colonel Vaon was killed by a German sniper, just a month before the end of the war.
72-year-old Michael Vaughan said that his grandfather's deeds were "something I know I can never be equal, but on which we are proud of."
He and his brother Dr. James Vaughn also garlanded after the parade, led by Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vaughan said that they are "very honored to play a role in the parade" and he said "the valory of a real hero is being demonstrated with the support being given by many people."
