Germany's new warship has been postponed again.
New warships of the German Navy should have been commissioned to replace time-influenced cold-war-era warships in 2014, but due to faulty systems and rapidly increasing costs, at least this will not happen until next year, local media reported.
The commissioning of "Reinland-Puffelz", which is the flagship ship of the all-new Baden-Würtembourg-category warships, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019.
The vessel should have been inducted into the Navy in 2014, but the disturbing issues after delivery affected the fate of the ambitious project.
Four Baden-Würtembourg-class ships were returned by the Navy in 2007 that they would be brought as a replacement for long-influenced Bremen-category warships.
It is understood that they will facilitate an array of powerful cannons, anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some stealth technology such as low radar, infrared and acoustic division.
Other important features include a long-term maintenance period — it is possible to deplocate the latest warships for up to two years from indigenous ports.
However, constant delays mean that state-of-the-art warships — which could give Germany a chance for a power project overseas — will be timeless until a period of entry into service, Dia Zeen commented.
The Durdanta F125 warship made headlines last year after the German Navy officially refused to commission the vessel and returned the Blohm and vos shipyard in Hambarg.
This was the first time the navy had returned a ship to the ship's builder after delivery.
Very few people were aware of the reasons for the withdrawal, but the German media cited many important "software and hardware defects" that rendered the warship useless when it was deployed on the mission of the war.
Software deficiencies were particularly important as the Baden-Würtembourg-category will be powered by a crew of some 120 sailors — which is just half of the manpower of old Bremain-class warships.
In addition, it also emerged that the ship is dramatically overweight, which reduces its performance and limits the Navy's ability to add future upgrades.
"Rinland-Puffelz" of 7,000-tons is considered twice heavier than the same-class ships used by the Germans in World War II.
In addition to faulty hardware, the price tag of the entire project — which also involves crew training — is becoming an issue.
It's said it's reached €3.1 billion ($3.6bn) from the initial €2.2 billion.
The problem of new warships, which became particularly important in the light of recent warnings, is gripping Germany's naval power.
"Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the German Parliament's Defense Committee, acknowledged that in the navy is actually "daunting."
The official said that the issue has become serious over time, as the old ships were removed from use, but no replacement ships were made available.
He said that none of the Baden-W�ürtemberg-class warships were able to join the navy.
The National Trust is secretly listening to the hidden life of bats
New research being carried out in a Scottish Highlands estate aims to disclute how bats use the natural landscape in their prey for food.
It is expected that the findings will shed new light on the behavior of the flying unique mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
The study, carried out by scientists at the National Trust of Scotland, will follow general and soprano pipistrals, as well as brown long-eared dobbenton bats located in the Inverway Garden of Weston Ross.
Special recorders will be set up at prominent places around the estate to track the activities of bats throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using handmade detectors.
The expert sound analysis of all the recordings shows the frequency of the bat call and which species are doing.
The natural-resistance map and report will then be prepared for the depiction on a detailed scenario-scale of their behavior.
Robb Devar, Nature Conservation Consultant of NTS, has the results will reveal which areas of natural-was are the most important for bats and how they are used by each species.
This information will help determine the benefits of natural-vass management tasks such as the construction of grasslands and how to maintain woodlands well for bats and other related species.
Bat population in Scotland and across the U.K. has declined significantly in the last century
They are threatened by construction and development works, which affect the bases of the birds and damage the natural habits.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, as some chemical treatments of flypapers and building materials, as well as attacks by pet cats can become a threat.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to their hunting habits when it comes to catching the prey, their ears are more useful than their eyes.
They use a sophisticated eco-location technique to indicate defects and obstacles in their flight path.
NTS, which is responsible for taking care for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes the bats very seriously.
It has ten-trained specialists, who regularly survey, observe birds' bases and sometimes rescue them.
The organization has established Scotland's first and only dedicated Bat reserve in the threeve estates of Dumfreeze and Galloway of Scotland, which is home to eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
Estate manager David Thompson says estate is a great place for him.
"Here we have a great area for bats in Threeves," he said.
"""We have old buildings, many old trees and all good natural habitats."
But there's a lot about bats that is still unknown, so the work we do here and in other estates will help us to understand what they need to grow."
They emphasize the importance of checking the bats before maintaining within the estates because it is possible that 400 females and infants can be destroyed by inadvertentiously devastation of a maternity obstetric base, which will probably wipe out the entire local population.
Bats are preserved and it is illegal to kill, persecute or harass or destroy their bases.
Elizabeth Ferrell, Scottish officer of the Bat Conservation Trust, has encouraged the public to come forward to help.
He said: "We still have a lot to get to know our bats and we still have no idea about many of our species how their population is growing."
