Brexit: U.K will always regret losing a carmaker
Trade Secretary Greig Clark said the U.K. would "remorse forever" if he loses his dominance as a world leader in car building after Braxit.
He said Toyota U.K. had told the BBC that it would temporarily stop production at its factory in Bernstone near Derby if Britain left the European Union.
"We need a deal," Mr. Clarke said.
The Japanese carmaker said the border delay could have an impact on jobs in the event of no-deal Brexit.
The Burnston Plant - which makes Toyota's Auris and avensis — produced about 150,000 cars last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union.
"I believe that if the UK exits the EU at the end of March, we will stop production at our factory," said Marvin Cook, managing director of Toyota in Burnstone.
Other U.K. carmakers have expressed apprehensions about leaving the EU without agreeing how cross-border trade will work, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
For example, BMW says it will shut down its mini plant for a month after Brexit in Oxford.
According to carmakers, the main concerns are related to the risk of the supply series in the event of no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's production line is run on a "just-in-time" basis, with both the U.K. and the European Union, coming from suppliers of made-to-order cars every 37 minutes.
If the U.K. leaves the European Union without a deal on March 29, there may be disruption on the border, according to the industry that could lead to delays and a shortage of spares.
The company said it would be impossible for Toyota to have an inventory of more than a day at its Derbyshire plant, so the production will be discontinued.
Mr. Clerk said Theresa May's checkers plan for future relations with the European Union is "calibrated to avoid those checks on the bround completely."
"We need a deal. We want a great deal I think can not only enjoy success at present, but which is also necessary to achieve this opportunity," he told BBC Radio 4's Today program.
"Not only found evidence from Toyota but other manufacturers that we must be able to fully continue that has been a highly successful set of supply series."
Toyota was unable to say how long the production would be discontinued, but in the long-term, warned that additional costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and would eventually lose jobs.
Peter Séwellerius, who has worked at Burnstone for 24 years and is the convener of the United Union at the plant, said their members are highly concerned: "Once these jobs are lost in my experience, they never come back in Baid."
A government spokesperson said: "We have pursued an accurate and reliable plan for our future relations with the European Union."
