How the US-China trade war can be good for Beijing
The initial salvo of the trade war between the U.S. and while the war is still far from the end, the rifts between the countries may be beneficial for Beijing in the long term, the experts said.
US President Donald Trump said that the first warning was given earlier this year by imposing major Chinese export taxes, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant increase this week, with new tariffs by affecting items up to $200 billion (£150 billion), is effectively taxed on more than half of the goods that came from China to the U.S.
Beijing has retaliated in its way, the most recent slapping tariffs of five to 10 per cent of $60 billion of U.S. goods.
China has pledged to reconciliate the U.S. shot-to-shot and the world's second-biggest economy is likely to shimmer soon.
Getting back to Washington means hollowing out the demands, but publicly bowing before the U.S. will be very embarrassing for Zee Jingping, China's president.
Nevertheless, experts say that Beijing can make its moves, with the U.S. trade war positively supporting China over the long-term, reducing the interdependence of two economies.
"The fluct political decision in Washington or Beijing could create conditions that either initiate economic tailspins in the country, which is actually even more dangerous than it has actually been seen by the audience before," said Abigail Grace, a research associate who focuss on Asia at the new U.S. security, at the center for a think-tank.
Syria 'preparady' to return refugees, Foreign Minister Says
Syria said it was ready for a voluntary withdrawal of refugees and is calling for help to re-create a country devastated by a more than seven-year-long war.
Speaking to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Maulam said that the situation in the country is improving.
"The situation on the land is more stable and safer due to the progress made in opposition to terrorism today," he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate the areas destroyed by the terrorists to re-establish a normal situation.
Due to terrorism, all the circumstances are now present for the voluntary return of refugees in the country and unilateral economic measures that target their daily lives and livelihoods.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled from the country since the start of the war in 2011.
The other six million people living in the country still need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Maulam said the Syrian regime would welcome help rebuilding the devastated country.
But he stressed that it will not accept conditional assistance or assistance from countries sponsoring the revolt.
